    {
      "id": "GENUS_PHAENOPLANA",
      "name": "Phaenoplana",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Phaenoplana — a genus of turbellarias.",
      "lineage": "Phaenoplana",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "SP_PHAENOPLANA_CAETARIA_P_REZ_GARC_A_NORE_A_CERVERA_2018",
          "name": "Phaenoplana caetaria Pérez-García, Noreña & Cervera, 2018",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Phaenoplana",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "Stylochoplanidae with elongated body, wider anteriorly, and folded within the margins. Length between 24 mm and 13 mm (19.3 ± 5.69). Colour of the dorsal surface light brown with yellowish spots (Fig. 15 A, C). Ventral surface pale brown. Tentacles absent. Tentacular and cerebral eyes arranged in two clusters, with approximately 15 tentacular eyes and 27 cerebral eyes in each cluster (Fig. 15 B, D). Pharynx ruffled, positioned slightly anteriorly, between the first and second thirds of the body. Opening of the mouth in the last third of the pharynx. Genital pores separated. Male copulatory apparatus composed of a true seminal vesicle, an interpolated prostatic vesicle, and a penis papilla with a penis rod. Female complex consists of a well-developed external vagina (vagina bulbosa) and reduced Lang’s vesicle (for more details, see Pérez-García et al. 2019).",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:9475097"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PHAENOPLANA_CHALLENGERI",
          "name": "Phaenoplana challengeri",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Phaenoplana",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "",
          "sourcedFrom": "none"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PHAENOPLANA_CONOCERAEA",
          "name": "Phaenoplana conoceraea",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Phaenoplana",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "",
          "sourcedFrom": "none"
        },
        {
          "id": "SP_PHAENOPLANA_KOPEPE_OYA_KAJIHARA_2019",
          "name": "Phaenoplana kopepe Oya & Kajihara, 2019",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Phaenoplana",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "Live specimens 17 – 21 mm (21 mm in holotype) in length, 5.5 – 7 mm (7 mm in holotype) in width. Body thin, elongate oval, narrow toward posterior end. Ground body color translucent to whitish opaque. General appearance of body light brown (Fig. 2 A, B). Dorsal body tinged with light brown due to minute granules scattered over entire surface except around margin. Dorsal surface of body around pharynx brown. Body margin translucent. Pair of tentacles present at between one-fifth to one-sixth body length (3.4 – 3.5 mm, 3.5 mm in holotype) from anterior end (Fig. 2 A); each tentacle containing at least 30 eyespots (Fig. 2 C). Cerebral eye clusters arranged along median line and congregated anterior to tentacles, consisting of 29 – 44 eyespots (29 in right cluster, 30 in left cluster in holotype, Fig. 2 D). Pharynx whitish, ruffled in shape, occupying twoninths to one-third of body length (3.7 – 7 mm, 7 mm in holotype), located slightly anteriorly to center of body (Fig. 2 B). Mouth opening ventrally at near posterior end of pharyngeal cavity (Fig. 3 A). Intestine highly branched and not anastomosing, spreading throughout body except margin and brain region. Pair of whitish sperm ducts and oviducts visible through ventral body wall (Fig. 2 B). Male and female gonopores separate; male gonopore opening at about one-third of body length (5.6 – 7 mm, 7 mm in holotype) from posterior end; female gonopore situated 0.5 – 0.7 mm (0.7 mm in holotype) posterior to male gonopore (Fig. 2 B).",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:10462152"
        },
